course: dictionary
lesson: 6184
title: Mirth and the power of laughter
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mirth is an old-fashioned word for laughter, fun and great happiness, especially when a group of people are laughing together. Today we would more often say «laughter» or «fun».
explain: Read the words around it. In «O great wizard, what is the sense of returning to me my Palace if at the same time you slay me with mirth!», the speaker says the wizard would slay him, which means kill, with mirth. So mirth must be something so strong that it could almost finish someone off.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it in: «you slay me with laughter!» still makes sense, so mirth means laughter here. Mirth is a noun, a naming word for a feeling, and you cannot count it, so you say «much mirth», not «many mirths».
fact: Mirth is a noun, the name of a feeling.
fact: Mirth means laughter, fun and great happiness, especially among a group of people.
fact: Mirth is uncountable, so you say «much mirth», not «many mirths».
fact: «Roars of mirth» means loud, big laughter from a crowd.
fact: Mirth is an old, literary word; in speech today we usually say «laughter» or «fun».
exercise: «O great wizard, what is the sense of returning to me my Palace if at the same time you slay me with mirth!» What does mirth mean here? | *Laughter | Anger | Sleep | Hunger
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as mirth in everyday speech? | Quiet | *Fun | Hurry | Rain
exercise: Which sentence uses mirth correctly? | The room was full of mirth as the children laughed together. | Mirth the room was full of children. | The mirths were loud in the room. | *The room was full of much mirth as the children laughed together.
test: Is mirth countable or uncountable? | Both, depending on the sentence | Neither, because it is a verb | Countable, so we say two mirths | *Uncountable, so we say much mirth
test: Which word in «slay me with mirth» is a verb meaning kill? | with | *slay | me | mirth
exam: The whole crowd erupted in mirth when the clown tripped into the pond. What is the best meaning of mirth here? | *Laughter and fun | Sadness | Anger | Silence
